WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.818 --> 00:00:05.651
- When I first started
climbing, I was so bad.

00:00:06.494 --> 00:00:08.545
I was so terrible, so slow.

00:00:08.545 --> 00:00:10.914
I'd tear my skin up all the time.

00:00:10.914 --> 00:00:12.261
And I was like, how the heck are we going

00:00:12.261 --> 00:00:15.676
to go and do Mount Kilimanjaro?

00:00:15.676 --> 00:00:17.843
Three words: I don't know.

00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:21.081
Those three words, three of the most

00:00:21.081 --> 00:00:22.593
important words in my life.

00:00:22.593 --> 00:00:25.315
All discoveries happen from I don't know.

00:00:25.315 --> 00:00:27.815
(piano music)

00:00:30.365 --> 00:00:34.029
My name is Kyle Maynard, and
I have an extremely hard time

00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:37.029
saying what it is exactly that I do.

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:42.490
I've been an athlete my entire life.

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:46.031
Football, to wrestling, to
weight-lifting, and Brazilian

00:00:46.031 --> 00:00:48.472
jiu jitsu, and now, most
recently, taking on climbing

00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:52.055
some of the highest
mountains in the world.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.432
I was born with congenital
amputation, so basically it

00:00:55.432 --> 00:00:59.498
means my arms end at the elbows,
my legs end at the knees.

00:00:59.498 --> 00:01:01.650
You know, I think some people,
they look at me and maybe

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.434
even feel sorry for me, but
really, truthfully, my life

00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:07.801
doesn't look a whole lot
different than yours.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:10.342
You know, I type on the
computer the same way,

00:01:10.342 --> 00:01:12.655
You know, I use my phone, just like anybody else.

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:16.822
I'm on Bumble like anybody, 
any other single person my age.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.241
My first experience with
climbing really anything

00:01:23.241 --> 00:01:28.168
of significance was in a
CrossFit competition in 2010.

00:01:28.168 --> 00:01:29.829
The first workout was you had
to do a thousand meter row

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:31.970
on this rowing machine and
a sprint up Stone Mountain.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:35.570
Everybody else did it in maybe 25 minutes.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.490
It took me an hour and 46 minutes.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.022
I tore all the skin up on my
arms, but I got to the top,

00:01:40.022 --> 00:01:43.414
and I was like, “Wow, this
is breathtakingly beautiful.”

00:01:43.414 --> 00:01:45.084
And I told my friend that night, I said,

00:01:45.084 --> 00:01:46.956
“I want to climb Mount Kilimanjaro.”

00:01:46.956 --> 00:01:48.672
I don't know if I can;
I don't know if I can't,

00:01:48.672 --> 00:01:51.881
but I do know that I want
to try to figure it out.

00:01:51.881 --> 00:01:55.631
I want to go and find
a way and get up there.

00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:01.371
There were a ton of challenges
with getting into climbing,

00:02:01.371 --> 00:02:03.868
and the biggest one was the gear.

00:02:03.868 --> 00:02:05.394
I literally started with
bath towels duct taped

00:02:05.394 --> 00:02:07.311
to my arms and my feet.

00:02:08.664 --> 00:02:09.542
'Cause I couldn't go to the store and buy

00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:10.947
a typical pair of shoes,
so we had to come up

00:02:10.947 --> 00:02:12.864
with a unique solution.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:19.397
Now to climb, I use a carbon
fiber special custom shoe.

00:02:21.954 --> 00:02:25.858
I used those to bear crawl up Kilimanjaro.

00:02:25.858 --> 00:02:28.207
(cheers)

00:02:28.207 --> 00:02:30.683
Then, me and my team, we took
on climbing Mount Aconcagua,

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:33.516
the highest peak in South America.

00:02:34.996 --> 00:02:38.496
I felt my body was in full shut-down mode.

00:02:40.598 --> 00:02:41.929
Man, it was brutal at moments,

00:02:41.929 --> 00:02:45.543
but it was breathtakingly beautiful.

00:02:45.543 --> 00:02:48.481
At that top of the mountain, you know,
you can almost make out

00:02:48.481 --> 00:02:51.096
and see the curvature of the earth.

00:02:51.096 --> 00:02:53.199
I mean, it was just a
moment I'll never forget.

00:02:53.199 --> 00:02:55.282
(cheers)

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:07.727
The reasons I climb aren't that
different from other people.

00:03:07.727 --> 00:03:10.341
95% of it does suck.

00:03:10.341 --> 00:03:11.494
I'm staring at the dirt.

00:03:11.494 --> 00:03:13.379
I'm not even able to go
and talk to my friends

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:15.751
and see the beautiful views unless I stop

00:03:15.751 --> 00:03:17.997
and sit down and look,
but that five percent

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:20.664
that doesn't suck, it's amazing.

00:03:22.628 --> 00:03:24.540
Getting to a place that
there's no way my wheelchair

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.472
could have brought me
there, that's what I love.

00:03:27.472 --> 00:03:28.929
I'm not trying to live to be a thousand.

00:03:28.929 --> 00:03:32.880
Like, I want to go out there and
have my life mean something.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.721
When I've gotten down and
I've looked at the mountain,

00:03:34.721 --> 00:03:37.720
and I see what we've
achieved, it's like: “Wow!” 

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.042
Maybe that was a really bad
idea that we were there,

00:03:40.042 --> 00:03:41.792
but it was beautiful.

